# Mechanism: from-url — HTTPS release/asset → declared path.
# Format: <dest>  <url>  sha256=<64-hex>  [requires=java,...]
# <dest> is repo-root-relative unless it starts with ~ or /.
#
# sha256= is MANDATORY on every row and the realizer refuses a row
# without one (matching from-elan and from-autotools-tarball). A URL is
# a name, not a pin: GitHub release assets can be re-uploaded under an
# unchanged tag, so only the digest says which bytes you got.
#
# Two rows lived here until 081M001E114087G0R001AZF4KD and both were
# deleted rather than checksummed, because nothing read what they wrote:
#
#   tools/tla/tla2tools.jar   ← the runners load src/Core.TLA/tla2tools.jar
#   tools/alloy/alloy.jar     ← the runners load src/Core.Alloy/alloy.jar
#
# Both verifier jars are COMMITTED to git (#8053), so the toolchain is
# byte-pinned by the diff and no install-time fetch is involved. The TLA
# row also demonstrated the re-upload hazard first-hand: its URL is
# tagged v1.8.0 but that tag is a rolling PRERELEASE whose tla2tools.jar
# asset was last re-uploaded 2026-08-11, serving a build three months
# newer than the jar the gate actually runs.
#
# Before adding a row, answer both: (1) what reads this path, and
# (2) what digest pins these bytes. A fetch that fails either question
# is attack surface, not a dependency.
